{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfsQGUEWidxPeZrxPoAyD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017woysxUosNEVLf8m5TEG9A","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-834\\docs\\reviews\\wi-834\\017-ADJUDICATE-1003705.md","content":"# WI-834 adjudication 017: dispute R16F1 (range ed2533cb..e6717f0b)\n\nScope bound: PROCESS.md §6 \"Review threat model\". Review covers a normal\nworking environment: content agents write, supported configurations, and\nregressions of supported behaviour. It excludes contrived reproductions and\nmalice (\"bugs and fail-open, not malice\").\n\n## R16F1: a PowerShell assignment hides its command from the blackout hook\n\n**What I observed.** I called `coordinator_guard.launch_reason`, the function\nthe PreToolUse hook denies from, on this repository's own `docs/agents.toml`\n(model CLIs `claude`, `codex`) at HEAD 1003705d.\n\n| PowerShell command | Decision | Segments |\n|---|---|---|\n| `claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$response = claude -p x` | allow | `[['$response', '=', 'claude', '-p', 'x']]` |\n| `[string]$r = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$a, $b = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$r += claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$env:X = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$r = (claude -p x)` | deny | |\n| `$r = & claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$r = $(claude -p x)` | deny | |\n| `$r = ... \\| claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$r = 'claude -p x'` | allow (correctly) | |\n| `$r = \"claude -p x\"` | allow (correctly) | |\n\nBash `r=$(claude -p x)` is denied. The cause is `_segment_command`, which\nskips only POSIX `NAME=value` words, so the PowerShell assignment target\n`$response` is taken as the command.\n\nIn a real PowerShell session, `$r = cmd /c echo LAUNCHED-RHS`, `[string]$s =\n...` and `$a, $b = ...` each ran the right-hand command and captured its\noutput. A single-quoted right-hand side stayed literal. The builder's account\nof the code holds.\n\n**Scope.** Unlike R4F1 (sitting 005), the reproduction needs no contrived\ninput:\n- `$x = some-command args` is PowerShell's ordinary way to capture a command's\n  output, the direct counterpart of Bash `r=$(...)`, which the hook already\n  denies.\n- An agent capturing a model's answer would write exactly this.\n\nSo it is content agents write in a normal environment, and in scope.\n\nIt also breaks the declared coverage. The Part B Done-when and LLR-300 promise\ndenial of \"a `Bash` or `PowerShell` call naming a model CLI as any command\nword, including after ... `VAR=value` prefixes\". In\n`$response = claude -p x`, `claude` is the command word PowerShell runs. The\nhook is supervision rather than enforcement (ruling (f)), but a supervision\nrule that misses the ordinary capture form fails open on normal use, which §6\ncounts as a bug.\n\n**Cost.** The fix stays at the one shell-reading boundary, where the earlier\nrounds placed this class:\n- the PowerShell dialect skips a leading assignment target and operator, and\n  reads the next unquoted, non-variable word as the command;\n- a quoted right-hand side stays data (`$r = 'claude'` must not become a false\n  denial, so quotedness must survive the reading);\n- regression cases are added to TC-339's evidence.\n\nThe builder estimates about 30 to 45 lines plus tests. That is small next to a\nblackout fail-open on a common form. The row texts (LLR-300's \"command word\",\nTC-339) already describe the intended behaviour, so this should need no\nre-attestation unless their wording changes.\n\nRULING: R16F1 FIX the PowerShell reading takes an assignment target as the command word, so `$response = claude -p x` (and `[type]$r =`, `$a, $b =`, `+=`, `$env:X =`) launches a model through the armed blackout hook, while plain `claude -p x` and `r=$(claude -p x)` are denied; this is ordinary output capture inside the declared \"any command word\" coverage, not evasion, and the fix belongs in the one shell-reading boundary with quoted right-hand values kept as data\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":453634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":550},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d8d1fbc-6a42-4a02-9ec0-8f8d2b61c24c","uuid":"f1367ffe-8c1a-4b84-bbf6-c588a6c17ae7","timestamp":"2026-10-09T21:50:33.413Z","request_id":"req_011CfsQGTxeXwfuUwUNZ1Cjj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017woysxUosNEVLf8m5TEG9A":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-834\\docs\\reviews\\wi-834\\017-ADJUDICATE-1003705.md","content":"# WI-834 adjudication 017: dispute R16F1 (range ed2533cb..e6717f0b)\n\nScope bound: PROCESS.md §6 \"Review threat model\". Review covers a normal\nworking environment: content agents write, supported configurations, and\nregressions of supported behaviour. It excludes contrived reproductions and\nmalice (\"bugs and fail-open, not malice\").\n\n## R16F1: a PowerShell assignment hides its command from the blackout hook\n\n**What I observed.** I called `coordinator_guard.launch_reason`, the function\nthe PreToolUse hook denies from, on this repository's own `docs/agents.toml`\n(model CLIs `claude`, `codex`) at HEAD 1003705d.\n\n| PowerShell command | Decision | Segments |\n|---|---|---|\n| `claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$response = claude -p x` | allow | `[['$response', '=', 'claude', '-p', 'x']]` |\n| `[string]$r = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$a, $b = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$r += claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$env:X = claude -p x` | allow | |\n| `$r = (claude -p x)` | deny | |\n| `$r = & claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$r = $(claude -p x)` | deny | |\n| `$r = ... \\| claude -p x` | deny | |\n| `$r = 'claude -p x'` | allow (correctly) | |\n| `$r = \"claude -p x\"` | allow (correctly) | |\n\nBash `r=$(claude -p x)` is denied. The cause is `_segment_command`, which\nskips only POSIX `NAME=value` words, so the PowerShell assignment target\n`$response` is taken as the command.\n\nIn a real PowerShell session, `$r = cmd /c echo LAUNCHED-RHS`, `[string]$s =\n...` and `$a, $b = ...` each ran the right-hand command and captured its\noutput. A single-quoted right-hand side stayed literal. The builder's account\nof the code holds.\n\n**Scope.** Unlike R4F1 (sitting 005), the reproduction needs no contrived\ninput:\n- `$x = some-command args` is PowerShell's ordinary way to capture a command's\n  output, the direct counterpart of Bash `r=$(...)`, which the hook already\n  denies.\n- An agent capturing a model's answer would write exactly this.\n\nSo it is content agents write in a normal environment, and in scope.\n\nIt also breaks the declared coverage. The Part B Done-when and LLR-300 promise\ndenial of \"a `Bash` or `PowerShell` call naming a model CLI as any command\nword, including after ... `VAR=value` prefixes\". In\n`$response = claude -p x`, `claude` is the command word PowerShell runs. The\nhook is supervision rather than enforcement (ruling (f)), but a supervision\nrule that misses the ordinary capture form fails open on normal use, which §6\ncounts as a bug.\n\n**Cost.** The fix stays at the one shell-reading boundary, where the earlier\nrounds placed this class:\n- the PowerShell dialect skips a leading assignment target and operator, and\n  reads the next unquoted, non-variable word as the command;\n- a quoted right-hand side stays data (`$r = 'claude'` must not become a false\n  denial, so quotedness must survive the reading);\n- regression cases are added to TC-339's evidence.\n\nThe builder estimates about 30 to 45 lines plus tests. That is small next to a\nblackout fail-open on a common form. The row texts (LLR-300's \"command word\",\nTC-339) already describe the intended behaviour, so this should need no\nre-attestation unless their wording changes.\n\nRULING: R16F1 FIX the PowerShell reading takes an assignment target as the command word, so `$response = claude -p x` (and `[type]$r =`, `$a, $b =`, `+=`, `$env:X =`) launches a model through the armed blackout hook, while plain `claude -p x` and `r=$(claude -p x)` are denied; this is ordinary output capture inside the declared \"any command word\" coverage, not evasion, and the fix belongs in the one shell-reading boundary with quoted right-hand values kept as data\n"}}}
